veloper/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9f525c4d5985d6aa8793a8be364b558d2b13f59fa68f5eb3b50e0d77a796da" "basis" 1255 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-143058" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{H},\\,k>0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9f525c4d5985d6aa8793a8be364b558d2b13f59fa68f5eb3b50e0d77a796da" "basis" 1255 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-119054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The amount of money $M(t)$ in a saving account increases at a rate that is proportional to the amount of money currently in the account. What differential equation describes the rate of change in the amount of money in the saving account $M,$ at time $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a3fb445dedee98736c8722f34b39bd97162cee6050a2cd720a0f4c5f2b1e0eb"} {"question" "q-119054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **amount of money** $M(t)$ in a saving account **increases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **amount of money** currently in the saving account.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *amount of money* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *amount of money.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}∝M.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the amount of money *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=kM.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a3fb445dedee98736c8722f34b39bd97162cee6050a2cd720a0f4c5f2b1e0eb"} {"question" "q-119054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b2878443cc0008e933413ef7a22fc8902a26adb7c74953e2c18905a7f30118ab"} {"question" "q-119054" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a3fb445dedee98736c8722f34b39bd97162cee6050a2cd720a0f4c5f2b1e0eb"} {"question" "q-119054" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}\\text{M}}{\\text{d}t}=-kM,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}\\text{M}}{\\text{d}t}=-k{M}^{2},\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}\\text{M}}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{M},\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}\\text{M}}{\\text{d}t}=k+M,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}\\text{M}}{\\text{d}t}=kM,\\,k>0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a3fb445dedee98736c8722f34b39bd97162cee6050a2cd720a0f4c5f2b1e0eb"} {"question" "q-119054" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}\\text{M}}{\\text{d}t}=kM,\\,k>0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a3fb445dedee98736c8722f34b39bd97162cee6050a2cd720a0f4c5f2b1e0eb"} {"question" "q-143035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "During a certain chemical reaction, the volume of gas $G$ increases with time. The rate of change of the volume of gas is proportional to the square of the time $t,$ in seconds, since the reaction started. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of gas at time $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a3fb445dedee98736c8722f34b39bd97162cee6050a2cd720a0f4c5f2b1e0eb"} {"question" "q-143035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*During a certain chemical reaction, the **volume** of gas $G$ **increases** with time. The **rate** of change of the volume of gas is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t,$ in seconds.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume of gas* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *square of the time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}∝{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of gas *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2}.\n