ance decreases with time. The rate of change of the substance's mass is proportional to the square of the mass. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the substance's mass at time $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a3fb445dedee98736c8722f34b39bd97162cee6050a2cd720a0f4c5f2b1e0eb"} {"question" "q-143038" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*During a chemical reaction, the **mass** $M$ of a particular substance  **decreases** with time. The **rate** of change of the substance's mass is **proportional** to the **square of the mass**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *mass* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *mass squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}∝{M}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the mass *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=-k{M}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a3fb445dedee98736c8722f34b39bd97162cee6050a2cd720a0f4c5f2b1e0eb"} {"question" "q-143038" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b2878443cc0008e933413ef7a22fc8902a26adb7c74953e2c18905a7f30118ab